-- ══ September 2026 (2026-09) ══
values ('2026-09', 'September', 2026, '2026-10-01', 60.1, 16312, 27141, 10829, 6785, 3262.4, 77, '60.38%', 9, 721, 463, 258, 451, 12, 350, 10, 10, 2255, 0, 0, 3937, -17362, 30, 241, 123, 17.3, 18, 0, 1.8, 53.2, 59, 50.8, 6201, 4388, 2108, 4388, 215, 385, 3490, 1346, '[{"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "September closed at 60.1% of target — 16,312 of 27,141 bags, 10,829 bags short. Unposted stock (4,388 bags) explains only 40.5% of that — the rest is sales pace."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "41.4% of Kenya stock (4,388 bags) was never posted — yet posted bags sold at 53.2% of expectation. Marketing is the lever, and much of it went unused.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Post the 41.4% of Kenya stock left unmarketed — posted bags already sell at 53.2% of expectation."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "Weekly output (~3,262 bags) ran below the 6,785/week floor needed to hit target — the problem is consistency, not a bad month.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Hold a hard 6,785/wk commit reviewed every Monday; recover ~1,761/wk to close the gap."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "Regions convert about the same: Kenya posts at 53.2% sales-achieved, Sinza 59.0%, Uganda 50.8%. Posting yields within 8 points of each other — the gap is what gets posted, not where.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Lift every region''s posting yield (best Sinza 59.0%) by pointing posts at bags that convert."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "Why stock isn''t moving, and how to sell it: 4,388 units were never posted (not seen) and 660 posted units sold under half their expected (seen, not bought). See section 5 for the October plan.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Post the biggest never-posted lines first; move the non-converters onto an offer; plan the rest for October."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "We finished September at 60.1% of target (16,312 bags), 10,829 bags short. Weekly output averaged about 3,262 bags — under the 6,785-bag weekly floor the target requires."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "Weekly output ran below the bare minimum needed to reach target most weeks. The shortfall is one of throughput and consistency, not demand — corporate orders (77 bags) only nudged the forecast to 60.38%."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "Closing even half the weekly gap (~1,761 bags/week) across the month adds ~7,045 bags — lifting achievement from 60.1% to ~86.1%."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "The 9 new products (LOOP BP, ZULA, LAMORA, TAJI, IMANI, NALA, AMORA, VOYAGE, LAFEMME) reached only 64.2% of their 721-bag target (463 sold) — a 258-bag deficit."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "New products received 350 marketing posts but converted just 463 bags, and almost all of it (451 of 463) was Kenya — outside-Kenya is effectively untested (12 bags). Awareness is being created; conversion and distribution are the bottleneck."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "Recovering half the deficit (~129 bags) is realistic within one cycle; a working outside-Kenya channel would add a comparable second stream."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "On offer?", "type": "insight", "text": "On offer (real cut): PRIME (-46%), ANTITHEFT (-32%), ELYSE (-40%), CODE 3 (-29%), AMAYA (-48%), MAN BAG (-38%), MINI MAYA (-17%), AURORA (-52%), FEROZ (-52%), JUMBO (-44%), MINI UMBRA (-38%), SAFIRI TRAVEL (-44%), ARIA SLING (-38%), AVANA HB (-41%), CATHY HANDBAG (-38%), KARINA (-40%), MINI ZURI (-47%), REMI (-26%), ACE (-38%), BIG MAN BAG (-29%), KAI (-29%), LEILA (-38%), LOLA (-29%), TRECENTO (-52%), KATE (-38%), LOOP BP (-44%), LUNCHSET (-38%), NIZANA (-17%), OVAL HANDBAG (-38%), SCHOOL BAG (-29%), ZANE MAN (-38%), ZIPPED LUNCHSET (-38%), ARIA PRO (-38%), ARLO MAN BAG (-38%), BELT BAG (-29%), BONITA (-49%), BUTTERFLY SLING (-38%), CELINE SLING BAG (-52%), CLAIRE HANDBAG (-33%), FABELA (-38%), FAYOLA (-52%), JADE (-52%), LUNA (-38%), MINI SCHOOL (-44%), MONAH BP (-25%), NEO MAN (-38%), PIONEER (-42%), POCKET TRAVEL (-31%), SARAI (-39%), ZENO (-38%), ZURI (-58%), CESS (-23%), LIAM (-19%), REO TRAVEL (-14%), TYLER (-15%), ZOEZI (-11%)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Stock", "type": "insight", "text": "Real inventory behind them — 599 still in stock (led by MAN BAG at 32). The offer draws down a genuine stock position, not a token one."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "On offer?", "type": "insight", "text": "All six were genuinely on offer — real list-price cuts of KES 300–300 (6–27% off): Cathy Handbag −300 (-15%), Reo Travel −300 (-11%), Cosmo −300 (-16%), Monah Bp −300 (-15%), Zula −300 (-14%), Loop Bp −300 (-11%), Leila −300 (-19%), Claire Handbag −300 (-10%), Spark −300 (-12%), Taji −300 (-10%), Scooby −300 (-12%), Zipped Lunchset −300 (-19%), Double Press −300 (-12%), Ace −300 (-19%), Alpha Travel −300 (-8%), Code 9 −300 (-14%), Fanny Pack −300 (-19%), Kaz −300 (-15%), Lamora −300 (-8%), Butterfly Sling −300 (-19%), Arlo Man Bag −300 (-19%), Tyler −300 (-12%), Feroz −300 (-10%), Legacy −300 (-9%), Lanka −300 (-17%), Don −300 (-19%), Mini Manbag −300 (-19%), Zelus −300 (-12%), Esmeralda −300 (-12%), Washbag −300 (-19%), Mini School −300 (-17%), Bello −300 (-12%), Arm Band −300 (-25%), Nyla Bp −300 (-10%), Umbra −300 (-14%), Marley −300 (-12%), Maya −300 (-19%), Wander Luxe −300 (-10%), Adrian −300 (-10%), Aria Pro −300 (-19%), Jade −300 (-10%), Lunchset −300 (-19%), College Hb −300 (-12%), Nala −300 (-6%), Zeno −300 (-19%), Pocket Travel −300 (-8%), Travolta −300 (-8%), Elektra −300 (-19%), Zuri −300 (-8%), Ankara Travel −300 (-12%), Chase −300 (-20%), Daria −300 (-19%), Imani −300 (-10%), Safiri Bp −300 (-12%), Callista −300 (-11%), Diaper Bag −300 (-7%), Luca −300 (-12%), Montana −300 (-20%), Nina −300 (-11%), Satchel −300 (-10%), Skye Hb −300 (-15%), Splash Backpack −300 (-14%), Vanity −300 (-27%)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Discount vs demand", "type": "insight", "text": "Because every bag was cut, the discount and back-to-school timing can''t be fully separated — both applied at once. But discount depth didn''t decide the winners: the deepest cut, Vanity (-27%), sold only 0, while Cathy Handbag led with 11 on a shallower -15% cut. The cut opened the door, but demand — which bags people wanted for school — chose what actually moved."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Stock", "type": "insight", "text": "Real inventory behind them — 2,878 still in stock (led by Double Press at 220). The offer draws down a genuine stock position, not a token one."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Season", "type": "insight", "text": "All six sit in Baby Bag, Backpack, Briefcase, Chest Bag, Handbag, Lunch Bag, Make Up, Man Bag, School Bag, Sling, Sport, Thigh Bag, Travel, Waist Bag, Washbag — school categories — and the step-up landed in the offer week as schools opened. Reo Travel and Cosmo even sold on zero marketing posts (pure demand). The timing fits the sales pattern as much as the price cut does."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "On offer?", "type": "insight", "text": "All six were genuinely on offer — real list-price cuts of KES 300–300 (6–27% off): Cathy Handbag −300 (-15%), Cosmo −300 (-16%), Zula −300 (-14%), Ace −300 (-19%), Reo Travel −300 (-11%), Kaz −300 (-15%), Zipped Lunchset −300 (-19%), Lamora −300 (-8%), Don −300 (-19%), Lunchset −300 (-19%), Alpha Travel −300 (-8%), College Hb −300 (-12%), Esmeralda −300 (-12%), Mini School −300 (-17%), Butterfly Sling −300 (-19%), Loop Bp −300 (-11%), Taji −300 (-10%), Claire Handbag −300 (-10%), Monah Bp −300 (-15%), Double Press −300 (-12%), Aria Pro −300 (-19%), Bello −300 (-12%), Travolta −300 (-8%), Umbra −300 (-14%), Scooby −300 (-12%), Tyler −300 (-12%), Feroz −300 (-10%), Legacy −300 (-9%), Lanka −300 (-17%), Spark −300 (-12%), Arm Band −300 (-25%), Code 9 −300 (-14%), Fanny Pack −300 (-19%), Zuri −300 (-8%), Imani −300 (-10%), Maya −300 (-19%), Wander Luxe −300 (-10%), Adrian −300 (-10%), Jade −300 (-10%), Mini Manbag −300 (-19%), Zelus −300 (-12%), Leila −300 (-19%), Washbag −300 (-19%), Nala −300 (-6%), Zeno −300 (-19%), Arlo Man Bag −300 (-19%), Pocket Travel −300 (-8%), Elektra −300 (-19%), Nyla Bp −300 (-10%), Ankara Travel −300 (-12%), Chase −300 (-20%), Daria −300 (-19%), Marley −300 (-12%), Safiri Bp −300 (-12%), Callista −300 (-11%), Diaper Bag −300 (-7%), Luca −300 (-12%), Montana −300 (-20%), Nina −300 (-11%), Satchel −300 (-10%), Skye Hb −300 (-15%), Splash Backpack −300 (-14%), Vanity −300 (-27%)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Discount vs demand", "type": "insight", "text": "Because every bag was cut, the discount and back-to-school timing can''t be fully separated — both applied at once. But discount depth didn''t decide the winners: the deepest cut, Vanity (-27%), sold only 0, while Cathy Handbag led with 14 on a shallower -15% cut. The cut opened the door, but demand — which bags people wanted for school — chose what actually moved."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Stock", "type": "insight", "text": "Real inventory behind them — 2,878 still in stock (led by Double Press at 220). The offer draws down a genuine stock position, not a token one."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Season", "type": "insight", "text": "All six sit in Baby Bag, Backpack, Briefcase, Chest Bag, Handbag, Lunch Bag, Make Up, Man Bag, School Bag, Sling, Sport, Thigh Bag, Travel, Waist Bag, Washbag — school categories — and the step-up landed in the offer week as schools opened. Cosmo and Zula even sold on zero marketing posts (pure demand). The timing fits the sales pattern as much as the price cut does."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "Across 10 combos and 10 power deals (Kenya), the two offer types cleared stock very differently: combos moved 2,255 bags (KES 0) while power deals moved 3,937 bags (KES -17,362). Power deals shifted the most stock; combos made the most money."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "It''s a price-point story: combos averaged KES 0 per offer vs power deals KES 30. The cheaper combos clear more volume, so dead stock moves fastest through combos — yet 2,973 bags remain in Kenya offer stock (plus 1,392 Sinza, 1,010 Uganda), so the mix still isn''t drawing inventory down fast enough."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Sinza & Uganda offers", "type": "insight", "text": "Sinza ran 10 combos, 10 singles and 0 specials, moving 241 bags (value 123) against 1,392 in stock. Uganda ran 3 combos and 0 singles, moving 18 bags (value 0) against 1,010 in stock. Both are clearing only a thin slice of their offer stock — the same dead-stock problem as Kenya, at smaller scale."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "Clearing just 20% of the 2,973-bag Kenya position (~595 bags) in the month is ~2.2% of target — and frees working capital tied up in slow stock."}, {"section": "Self-Made Combos", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "Staff-made combos were 178 combos moving 180 units (KES 927,898) — 15% of all combo volume — vs 10 official running combos moving 1,038 units (KES 4,654,824). 217 combo requests were logged this month. 3 request(s) were rejected (e.g. CBR/2026/0631 — Request by mistake)."}, {"section": "Self-Made Combos", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "Self-made combos average KES 5,155 vs running combos KES 4,484, so the running combos are the cheaper ticket. Every self-made combo needs an approved CBR, so the request table behind them is the audit trail — who requested what, from which shop, and whether it actually sold."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "Marketing posting is our most effective lever — Kenya posted bags hit 53.2% of expected sales monthly — but 41.4% of in-stock bags (4,388) were never posted. The target gap is sitting in unmarketed stock."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Which bags were never postedAgainst the month''s posting, these in-stock bags carry zero marketing posts — the dead stock marketing moves first so the sales team can reach target:Green FABELA GREEN97 in stock · 0 postsBrown MINI UMBRA CRACKED84 in stock · 0 postsBlack AURORA BLACK78 in stock · 0 postsGrey CODE 3 GREY78 in stock · 0 postsGrey FABELA GREY77 in stock · 0 postsBlack TRAVEL BLACK75 in stock · 0 postsGrey ARIA SLING GREY74 in stock · 0 postsBlack LIAM BLACK73 in stock · 0 postsBrown LIAM BROWN71 in stock · 0 postsBrown ARIA SLING CRACKED69 in stock · 0 posts The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "Where we post, we sell: monthly sales on posted bags (3,490) ran ~2.6× the 1,346 expected. Yet of 10,589 bags in Kenya stock, 4,388 got no post all month. Regionally the discipline collapses — Kenya 53.2%, Sinza 59.0%, Uganda 50.8% — so the biggest untapped demand is in Uganda and in unmarketed stock."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "If the 4,388 unposted bags converted at even a conservative 20% once marketed, that is ~878 bags — enough to close ~8.1% of the entire target gap. Bringing Uganda toward Kenya''s rate compounds it further."}, {"section": "Remaining Stock — Why It Isn''t Moving & How to Sell It", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "September has closed, so this is the opening plan for October. Kenya is holding 4,388 units that were never posted (105 bag lines) and 660 units that were posted but sold under half of what their posts should bring (42 lines). Posting alone can move ~878 of these early next month — so the question is which bags get the posts."}, {"section": "Remaining Stock — Why It Isn''t Moving & How to Sell It", "label": "Why — two different problems Not being seen: in stock, zero posts this month. These have never had a chance — posting is the fix. FABELA GREEN97 in stock · 0 postsMINI UMBRA CRACKED84 in stock · 0 postsAURORA BLACK78 in stock · 0 postsCODE 3 GREY78 in stock · 0 postsFABELA GREY77 in stock · 0 postsTRAVEL BLACK75 in stock · 0 postsARIA SLING GREY74 in stock · 0 postsLIAM BLACK73 in stock · 0 posts Seen but not bought: posted, yet sold under 50% of expected. More posts won''t fix these — price, colour or offer will. GYM BAG GREY51 in stock · 26 posts · 27 soldMINI ZURI GREY49 in stock · 23 posts · 23 soldLUNCHSET BLACK49 in stock · 21 posts · 14 soldNEO MAN GREY44 in stock · 36 posts · 21 soldLAMORA RED30 in stock · 40 posts · 9 soldMANDY HB BLACK25 in stock · 14 posts · 4 soldLAFEMME PINK (E''CLAT)25 in stock · 7 posts · 5 soldNALA BLUE24 in stock · 51 posts · 4 sold How — within the month At September''s pace of ~70 posts/day, each Kenya post brought in ~1.2 bags. Start October with the posts where they move the most stock: StartPost the biggest never-posted lines first (top of the red list) — they hold the most stock and have had no marketing at all. Start testingMove the \"seen but not bought\" lines onto an offer — a power deal, a combo slot (Offer Picking) or a price step — instead of re-posting them. StopSpending posts on bags that already sell without them, while these lines sit. Business Impact", "type": "insight", "text": "~5,048 units carry into October — schedule them week by week so they are posted or on an offer by mid-month."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Managing to a hard 6,785-bag weekly commit, reviewed every Monday — not a single monthly number reconciled at month-end."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Treating the ~3,523-bag weekly gap as normal. Each missed week is unrecoverable against a fixed monthly target."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "A concentrated push on the weakest launches with price/offer support to clear the 258-bag deficit."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Outside-Kenya distribution for the best-performing new lines — 12 bags sold there all month is an untested market, not a dead one."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Routing the slowest dead stock through combos (the higher-volume clearer) and keeping premium movers in power deals for margin."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Restocking the slowest-moving colours until the 2,973-bag Kenya position draws down."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Focus — move the bags NOT on offerMarketing''s real target is the stock sitting not on offer. Read across the three posting lenses:CombinedSales vs Expected (Posted & Unposted) — of Kenya''s in-stock bags, 6,201 are on offer but 4,388 sit not on offer: dead stock we aren''t even putting in front of buyers.With postingSales with Posting vs Expected — where marketing posted, sales ran ~2.6× expected. Posting demonstrably works, so aiming it at the not-on-offer pile is the lever.No postingSales with no Posting vs Opportunity if Marketed — the sales team already moved 4,278 bags with zero marketing; the gap up to the opportunity is what marketing leaves on the table by not posting the not-on-offer stock.Not-on-offer stock by region: Kenya 4,388, Sinza 215, Uganda 385 — and Sinza (59.0%) and Uganda (50.8%) move it far slower than Kenya (53.2%). Same playbook, all three regions. Bags NOT on offer, by region — the dead stock marketing must move Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Systematically posting the 4,388 unmarketed in-stock bags — the single largest, cheapest lever against the 10,829-bag target gap."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Focus — move the bags NOT on offerMarketing''s real target is the stock sitting not on offer. Read across the three posting lenses:CombinedSales vs Expected (Posted & Unposted) — of Kenya''s in-stock bags, 6,201 are on offer but 4,388 sit not on offer: dead stock we aren''t even putting in front of buyers.With postingSales with Posting vs Expected — where marketing posted, sales ran ~2.6× expected. Posting demonstrably works, so aiming it at the not-on-offer pile is the lever.No postingSales with no Posting vs Opportunity if Marketed — the sales team already moved 4,278 bags with zero marketing; the gap up to the opportunity is what marketing leaves on the table by not posting the not-on-offer stock.Not-on-offer stock by region: Kenya 4,388, Sinza 215, Uganda 385 — and Sinza (59.0%) and Uganda (50.8%) move it far slower than Kenya (53.2%). Same playbook, all three regions. Bags NOT on offer, by region — the dead stock marketing must move Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "The Sinza posting playbook in Uganda, where sales-achieved is 50.8% vs Sinza''s 59.0%."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Focus — move the bags NOT on offerMarketing''s real target is the stock sitting not on offer. Read across the three posting lenses:CombinedSales vs Expected (Posted & Unposted) — of Kenya''s in-stock bags, 6,201 are on offer but 4,388 sit not on offer: dead stock we aren''t even putting in front of buyers.With postingSales with Posting vs Expected — where marketing posted, sales ran ~2.6× expected. Posting demonstrably works, so aiming it at the not-on-offer pile is the lever.No postingSales with no Posting vs Opportunity if Marketed — the sales team already moved 4,278 bags with zero marketing; the gap up to the opportunity is what marketing leaves on the table by not posting the not-on-offer stock.Not-on-offer stock by region: Kenya 4,388, Sinza 215, Uganda 385 — and Sinza (59.0%) and Uganda (50.8%) move it far slower than Kenya (53.2%). Same playbook, all three regions. Bags NOT on offer, by region — the dead stock marketing must move Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Letting in-stock bags sit unposted — every unmarketed bag is a bag that reliably does not sell."}]'::jsonb, '{"deals": {"powerCount": 10, "powerSold": 4385, "powerRevenue": 7952198, "powerDisc": 4200, "powerSoldExCombo": 1960, "dowProducts": 63, "dowRows": 154, "dowLocations": 17, "dowSold": 1317, "dowRevenue": 2563500, "dowDisc": 25000, "dowSoldExCombo": 670, "dedupSold": 5637, "dedupRevenue": 10350898, "dedupProducts": 55, "overlapProducts": 18, "comboBagDeals": 21, "tiers": [{"tier": "1", "products": 31, "units": 367, "revenue": 748600, "discount": 146000, "weeksElapsed": 2, "inProgress": false, "runRate": 183.5, "label": "Tier 1", "window": "Wk 1–2"}, {"tier": "2", "products": 32, "units": 950, "revenue": 1732100, "discount": 369700, "weeksElapsed": 3, "inProgress": true, "runRate": 316.7, "label": "Tier 2", "window": "Wk 3–5"}], "power": [{"product": "Antitheft", "tier": "All", "orig": 2600, "now": 2200, "disc": 400, "sold": 1161, "revenue": 2549500, "stock": 244}, {"product": "Man Bag", "tier": "All", "orig": 1600, "now": 1200, "disc": 400, "sold": 988, "revenue": 1186100, "stock": 157}, {"product": "MINI MAYA", "tier": "All", "orig": 1600, "now": 1200, "disc": 400, "sold": 575, "revenue": 688800, "stock": 90}, {"product": "Standard travel", "tier": "All", "orig": 2600, "now": 2200, "disc": 400, "sold": 401, "revenue": 879400, "stock": 221}, {"product": "Safiri Travel", "tier": "All", "orig": 3600, "now": 3000, "disc": 600,